 {
  "source": "William Blair",
  "title": "Total Recall: How AI Is Supercharging Memory Demand",
  "type": "sector",
  "priority": "high",
  "published": "2026-05-21",
  "insights": [
   "AI performance is increasingly limited by the 'memory wall,' where compute FLOPS have scaled 3x every two years while DRAM bandwidth has only improved 1.6x, creating a 600:1 performance gap.",
   "HBM (High-Bandwidth Memory) is the primary solution to this bottleneck, with HBM4 expected to double bus capacity to 2,048 bits and exceed 2 TB/s per stack in 2026.",
   "Memory vendors are aggressively shifting capacity toward HBM, which commands 3-4x the ASP and significantly higher gross margins (55-65%) compared to traditional DRAM (25-45%).",
   "The HBM base logic die is becoming a critical value-capture battleground, with vendors like Micron and SK Hynix integrating memory controllers directly into the base die to enable deeper codesign with processor vendors.",
   "Inference workloads are driving a surge in NAND/SSD demand for persistent context storage, with Nvidia's new 'Inference Context Memory Storage' platform highlighting the role of SSDs as an active tier.",
   "Memory controllers and interface technologies are evolving into high-value components, benefiting vendors like Rambus, Silicon Motion, and Marvell as they manage increasingly complex heterogeneous connections.",
   "The report initiates coverage on Micron, Rambus, and Silicon Motion, citing their strategic positioning in the AI memory and controller ecosystem."
  ],
  "market_movers": [
   {
    "ticker": "MU",
    "action": "initiate",
    "rating": "Outperform",
    "price_target": "N/A",
    "rationale": "Leading HBM3E/HBM4 roadmap and strong traction with Nvidia and ASIC platforms.",
    "conviction": "high"
   },
